[{:db/id 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c445f3d3-7d56-5451-92cd-caec3d662075" "question-c95c5475-5100-54a8-b160-4567ec570e4d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186187993 :question/problem "Given that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{6}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(2,3^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq 6,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(10\\leq Y\\leq 14),$ where  $Y = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_{6}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6103 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6141 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6480 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6517 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6844 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6879 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_n\\sim N(n\\mu, \\: n\\sigma^2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{6}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{6} & ∼N(6\\cdot 2,\\,6\\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(6\\cdot 2,\\,6\\cdot 9) \\\\  & =N(12,54).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(10\\leq Y \\leq 14).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10\\le Y\\le 14) & =P(\\frac{10-12}{\\sqrt{54}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{14-12}{\\sqrt{54}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{54}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{54}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.27\\le Z\\le 0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)-Φ(-0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)-(1-Φ(0.27)) \\\\  & =2Φ(0.27)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.27) = 0.6064.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(10\\leq Y \\leq 14) = 2\\Phi(0.27) - 1 = 0.2128.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ca09d1d4-8ed3-5be9-8d9b-e96bfc72481d" "question-059f78b6-6d35-5bee-b74d-2081324d31e2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188213 :question/problem "In a library, each shelf is filled with $20$ volumes of an encyclopedia, plus one bookend. The weight of one volume, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $150\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\textrm g,$ while the weight of a bookend, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\textrm g.$ If one shelf is randomly selected, what is the probability that it supports a weight of more than $3\\,000\\,\\textrm g?$ You may assume that the weights of the volumes and bookend are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 2.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9778 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9783 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9788 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 2.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9821 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9826 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9830 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9834 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 2.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9861 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9864 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9868 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9871 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X_i$ denote the weight of the $i$th volume, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a bookend. Then, we have\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(150, 5^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq 20,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(50, 2^2).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected shelf. Then, since\n\n$$\nW = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_{20} + Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =20\\cdot 150+50 \\\\  & =3\\,050, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =20\\cdot {5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =504.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW \\sim N(3\\,050, 504).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W > 3\\,000),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>3\\,000) & =P(Z>\\frac{3\\,000-3\\,050}{\\sqrt{504}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-2.23) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.23) \\\\  & =Φ(2.23)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(2.23)\\approx 0.9871.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>3\\,000) & ≈Φ(2.23) \\\\  & =0.9871.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c445f3d3-7d56-5451-92cd-caec3d662075" :question/id #uuid "c445f3d3-7d56-5451-92cd-caec3d662075" :question/math-academy-id "q-152013" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(0.5,{0.3}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 16,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(8\\le Y\\le 9),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{16}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{16} & ∼N(16\\cdot 0.5,\\,16\\cdot {0.3}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(16\\cdot 0.5,\\,16\\cdot 0.09) \\\\  & =N(8,1.44).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(8\\le Y\\le 9).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8\\le Y\\le 9) & =P(\\frac{8-8}{\\sqrt{1.44}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{9-8}{\\sqrt{1.44}}) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{0}{1.2}\\le Z\\le \\frac{1}{1.2}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le 0.83) \\\\  & =Φ(0.83)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(0.83)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.83)=0.7967\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8\\le Y\\le 9) & =Φ(0.83)-0.5 \\\\  & =0.7967-0.5 \\\\  & =0.2967.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3788d99-c3b5-5c40-99f4-4ba2896c1904"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3788d99-c3b5-5c40-99f4-4ba2896c1904" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3788d99-c3b5-5c40-99f4-4ba2896c1904" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fcad6178-0bf1-554f-b2e3-145bb8f19eda" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80f477a4-5a83-5500-bcd7-f48756becfb4" "answer-a2d517d4-1cf5-5f97-aa0e-b240d249a6f1" "answer-29e4a75e-a311-5d28-9bf0-f84a736c11a0" "answer-fcad6178-0bf1-554f-b2e3-145bb8f19eda" "answer-43cc1043-6ae8-5d14-8170-f1ce77fb6d3c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80f477a4-5a83-5500-bcd7-f48756becfb4" :answer/id #uuid "80f477a4-5a83-5500-bcd7-f48756becfb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2642" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2d517d4-1cf5-5f97-aa0e-b240d249a6f1" :answer/id #uuid "a2d517d4-1cf5-5f97-aa0e-b240d249a6f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2673" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29e4a75e-a311-5d28-9bf0-f84a736c11a0" :answer/id #uuid "29e4a75e-a311-5d28-9bf0-f84a736c11a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2704" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcad6178-0bf1-554f-b2e3-145bb8f19eda" :answer/id #uuid "fcad6178-0bf1-554f-b2e3-145bb8f19eda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2967" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43cc1043-6ae8-5d14-8170-f1ce77fb6d3c" :answer/id #uuid "43cc1043-6ae8-5d14-8170-f1ce77fb6d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3238" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c95c5475-5100-54a8-b160-4567ec570e4d" :question/id #uuid "c95c5475-5100-54a8-b160-4567ec570e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143339" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(0,{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 15,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\ge 1),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15} & ∼N(15\\cdot 0,\\,15\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(15\\cdot 0,\\,15\\cdot \\frac{4}{25}) \\\\  & =N(0,\\frac{12}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge 1).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-0}{\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.65) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.65) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.65)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.65)=0.7422.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=1-Φ(0.65)=0.2578.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44ef41a7-faaf-5672-935c-ff24abe14152"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44ef41a7-faaf-5672-935c-ff24abe14152" :answer-field/id #uuid "44ef41a7-faaf-5672-935c-ff24abe14152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" "answer-693ff683-c441-5136-b7ec-abf843bb1296" "answer-0b1b048e-b282-560b-8713-335a9004952e" "answer-bcd73452-7201-5d58-8fb9-e272ec4a7b94" "answer-020bc723-6af1-5316-aaf4-e9de001b448a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer/id #uuid "e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2578" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-693ff683-c441-5136-b7ec-abf843bb1296" :answer/id #uuid "693ff683-c441-5136-b7ec-abf843bb1296" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2877" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b1b048e-b282-560b-8713-335a9004952e" :answer/id #uuid "0b1b048e-b282-560b-8713-335a9004952e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7123" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcd73452-7201-5d58-8fb9-e272ec4a7b94" :answer/id #uuid "bcd73452-7201-5d58-8fb9-e272ec4a7b94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7329" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-020bc723-6af1-5316-aaf4-e9de001b448a" :answer/id #uuid "020bc723-6af1-5316-aaf4-e9de001b448a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7422" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca09d1d4-8ed3-5be9-8d9b-e96bfc72481d" :question/id #uuid "ca09d1d4-8ed3-5be9-8d9b-e96bfc72481d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143408" :question/problem "The time it takes John to get to school, in minutes, is normally distributed with a mean of $20\\,\\text{min}$ and a standard deviation of $6\\,\\text{min}.$\nWhat is the probability that the sum of the times John takes to get to school on $5$ randomly selected school days is more than $90\\,\\text{min}?$\nYou may assume that the times of each day are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the time it took John to get to school on the $i$th day. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(20,{6}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 5.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the time John takes to get to school on $5$ randomly selected days. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{5},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =5\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =100, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =5\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(100,180).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>90),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>90) & =P(Z>\\frac{90-100}{\\sqrt{180}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.75) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)≈0.7734.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>90) & ≈0.7734.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a35070d4-02b5-5d30-b418-f640a67c5011"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a35070d4-02b5-5d30-b418-f640a67c5011" :answer-field/id #uuid "a35070d4-02b5-5d30-b418-f640a67c5011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-851b1aa9-452e-5b02-8786-868abe5b16f9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3121b90f-0af1-5d7b-81b3-a401dae948b6" "answer-38ccee7a-03a8-518f-922b-65abb94e2d9d" "answer-42557eb4-4a13-5772-9500-1fb4860965eb" "answer-a905c15b-eab1-5b75-b7fb-131f01bd69d7" "answer-851b1aa9-452e-5b02-8786-868abe5b16f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3121b90f-0af1-5d7b-81b3-a401dae948b6" :answer/id #uuid "3121b90f-0af1-5d7b-81b3-a401dae948b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2266" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38ccee7a-03a8-518f-922b-65abb94e2d9d" :answer/id #uuid "38ccee7a-03a8-518f-922b-65abb94e2d9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2578" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42557eb4-4a13-5772-9500-1fb4860965eb" :answer/id #uuid "42557eb4-4a13-5772-9500-1fb4860965eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2912" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a905c15b-eab1-5b75-b7fb-131f01bd69d7" :answer/id #uuid "a905c15b-eab1-5b75-b7fb-131f01bd69d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7422" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-851b1aa9-452e-5b02-8786-868abe5b16f9" :answer/id #uuid "851b1aa9-452e-5b02-8786-868abe5b16f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7734" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-059f78b6-6d35-5bee-b74d-2081324d31e2" :question/id #uuid "059f78b6-6d35-5bee-b74d-2081324d31e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-152023" :question/problem "In a plumbing store, the length of a water pipe is normally distributed with a mean of $2\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $0.05\\,\\text{m}.$ If Carl bought $25$ pipes, what is the probability that the total length of the pipes he purchased is more than $49.8\\,m$ and less $50.2\\,m?$ It can be assumed that the lengths of pipes are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the length in the $i$th pipe. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(2,{0.05}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 25.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $L$ denote the total length of $25$ randomly selected pipes. Then, since\n\n$$\nL={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{25},\n$$\n\nwe have that $L$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[L] & =25\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =50, \\\\ \\text{Var}[L] & =25\\cdot {0.05}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL∼N(50,0.0625).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(49.8<L<50.2),$ we convert $L$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.8<L<50.2) & =P(\\frac{49.8-50}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}<Z<\\frac{50.2-50}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.8<Z<0.8) \\\\  & =1-2P(Z>0.8) \\\\  & =1-2[1-P(Z<0.8)] \\\\  & =2P(Z<0.8)-1 \\\\  & =2Φ(0.8)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.8)≈0.7881.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.8<L<50.2) & ≈2Φ(0.8)-1 \\\\  & ≈2\\cdot 0.7881-1 \\\\  & ≈0.5762.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4e908b69-05cf-5c42-8fda-9f3d685c5ae8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4e908b69-05cf-5c42-8fda-9f3d685c5ae8" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e908b69-05cf-5c42-8fda-9f3d685c5ae8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5f0ea2b-d75e-5c32-8585-881bb10391dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16785778-8057-554c-8271-d87d44dd9e29" "answer-e5f0ea2b-d75e-5c32-8585-881bb10391dd" "answer-60af78be-1051-5303-9f10-219c33144a4b" "answer-abc985ed-2f5e-53c2-a58e-1ddd9c4a0a85" "answer-7e2b4c04-4b87-53b7-8666-c912cb081acc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16785778-8057-554c-8271-d87d44dd9e29" :answer/id #uuid "16785778-8057-554c-8271-d87d44dd9e29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5222" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5f0ea2b-d75e-5c32-8585-881bb10391dd" :answer/id #uuid "e5f0ea2b-d75e-5c32-8585-881bb10391dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5762" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60af78be-1051-5303-9f10-219c33144a4b" :answer/id #uuid "60af78be-1051-5303-9f10-219c33144a4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5878" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abc985ed-2f5e-53c2-a58e-1ddd9c4a0a85" :answer/id #uuid "abc985ed-2f5e-53c2-a58e-1ddd9c4a0a85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5934" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e2b4c04-4b87-53b7-8666-c912cb081acc" :answer/id #uuid "7e2b4c04-4b87-53b7-8666-c912cb081acc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6372" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189373 :tutorial/content "A set of random variables is said to be **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**) if the random variables are mutually independent and have the same probability distribution.\n\nWhen we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population, the sample elements\n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n\n$$\n\nare often considered to be I.I.D. random variables. This assumption often allows us to simplify the statistical analysis. \n\nSuppose that the random variables $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are I.I.D, where\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq n.\n$$\n\nLet's consider their sum\n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_n.\n$$\n\nSince each $X_i$ is normally distributed, it follows that $Y$ is normally distributed as well. Moreover, we can compute the mean and the variance of $Y$ as follows:\n\n- Calculating the mean using the properties of expectation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{E}[{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\mu +\\mu +⋯+\\mu  \\\\  & =n\\mu .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, using the properties of variance, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots+X_n\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2).\n$$\n\nWe will soon use this important result to develop models of how random samples are distributed. But for now, let's practice working with I.I.D normal random variables." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188454 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
